# Predicting income level from socio-demographic data
 
Individual assignment, Data Mining & Machine Learning course.
 
## Research question
 
Can we predict whether a person earns more than $50K a year from their socio-demographic characteristics, and which factors matter most?
 
## Data
 
The Adult Income dataset (US census data): 32,561 individuals and 15 variables such as age, education, occupation, marital status, sex, hours worked per week and capital gains. The target `income` was converted to a binary variable (1 if above $50K).
 
The classes are imbalanced: only 24% of individuals earn more than $50K.
 
## Exploratory analysis
 
- Education level (`education.num`) is the numerical variable most correlated with income (0.34), followed by age, hours worked and capital gains
- About 30% of men earn more than $50K, compared with 11% of women
- The median age is 44 for high earners and 34 for the others
- Most people work around 40 hours a week, with a few outliers above 60
## Models
 
Since the goal is to predict a class rather than a continuous value, I used two supervised classification models:
 
- **Logistic regression**, first with statsmodels to read the coefficients, then with scikit-learn (with class balancing) for prediction
- **Random forest** (100 trees, maximum depth 10)
Features: age, education level, capital gains and hours worked (standardised), plus marital status, occupation and sex (one-hot encoded). The data was split 80% training / 20% test.
 
## Results
 
| Model | Accuracy | Precision (>50K) | Recall (>50K) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Logistic regression | 80% | 57% | 83% |
| Random forest | 85% | 80% | 53% |
 
- The logistic regression has a pseudo-R² close to 0.40. Capital gains, being married and managerial or professional occupations increase the probability of a high income.
- The random forest is more accurate overall but misses almost half of the high earners, mainly because of the class imbalance.
- According to the random forest, the most important variables are marital status (24%), capital gains (20%) and education level (16%).
In short, logistic regression is the better tool to understand the role of each variable, while the random forest gives the best overall predictive performance.
 
## Files
 
| File | Content |
|---|---|
| `ProjectDM.ipynb` | Python notebook with the full analysis |
| `adultDB.csv` | Dataset, cleaned beforehand in Excel (separator `;`) |
 
Libraries: pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, statsmodels, matplotlib, seaborn. To run the notebook, change the path in `pd.read_csv()` to point to `adultDB.csv`.
